1
00:05:57,391 --> 00:06:03,391
A forbidden love. An unthinkable attraction. The ultimate price.

2
00:11:54,782 --> 00:12:00,782
Lolita is a drama, romance film directed by Adrian Lyne.

3
00:17:52,173 --> 00:17:58,173
Humbert Humbert is a middle-aged British novelist who is both appalled
by and attracted to the vulgarity of American culture.

4
00:23:49,565 --> 00:23:55,565
When he comes to stay at the boarding house run by Charlotte Haze, he soon
becomes obsessed with Lolita, the woman's teenaged daughter.

5
00:29:46,956 --> 00:29:52,956
Humbert Humbert, played by Jeremy Irons.

6
00:35:44,347 --> 00:35:50,347
Dolores "Lolita" Haze, played by Dominique Swain.

7
00:41:41,739 --> 00:41:47,739
Charlotte Haze, played by Melanie Griffith.

8
00:47:39,130 --> 00:47:45,130
Clare Quilty, played by Frank Langella.

9
00:53:36,521 --> 00:53:42,521
Miss Pratt, played by Suzanne Shepherd.

10
00:59:33,913 --> 00:59:39,913
Reverend Rigger, played by Keith Reddin.

11
01:05:31,304 --> 01:05:37,304
Lolita is a 1997 drama film directed by Adrian
Lyne and written by Stephen Schiff.

12
01:11:28,695 --> 01:11:34,695
It is the second screen adaptation of Vladimir Nabokov's 1955 novel of the same name and stars Jeremy Irons as Humbert Humbert and Dominique
Swain as Dolores "Lolita" Haze, with supporting roles by Melanie Griffith as Charlotte Haze and Frank Langella as Clare Quilty.

13
01:17:26,086 --> 01:17:32,086
The film is about a middle-aged professor who is sexually
attracted to adolescent girls he calls "nymphets".

14
01:23:23,478 --> 01:23:29,478
He rents a room in the house of a young widow to get closer to her
14-year-old daughter Dolores Haze "Lo", whom he calls "Lolita".

15
01:29:20,869 --> 01:29:26,869
Obsessed with the girl, he eventually gains control over her
after he takes her cross-country with him.

16
01:35:18,260 --> 01:35:24,260
Compared to Stanley Kubrick's 1962 version, Lyne's film is more overt with many of the novel's
darker elements; Kubrick chose to use suggestion and innuendo for comic purposes.

17
01:41:15,652 --> 01:41:21,652
Although praised by some critics for its faithfulness to Nabokov's narrative and the performances of
Irons and Swain, the film received a mixed critical reception in the United States.

18
01:47:13,043 --> 01:47:19,043
The film premiered in Europe in 1997 before being released in the United States in
1998 because it had difficulty finding an American distributor.

19
01:53:10,434 --> 01:53:16,434
It was eventually picked up by the cable network Showtime before finally
being released theatrically by The Samuel Goldwyn Company.

20
01:59:07,826 --> 01:59:13,826
Similarly, Lolita was met with much controversy in Australia, where it
was not given a theatrical release until April 1999.

21
02:05:05,217 --> 02:05:11,217
The film explores themes of jealousy, based on novel
or book, police, obsession, professor.

22
02:11:02,608 --> 02:11:08,608
The tone is emotional.
